---
// /hosting/limits/: GitHub's limits (file, push, repository, tree, push policy, Pages), the registry's
// own caps, and who pays for what (D00-9). A static page (0 requests), science.css only; the
// platform's name from src/config.ts. Every figure comes from GITHUB_LIMITS (worker/forge/limits.ts)
// and the registry's caps (worker/forge/service/caps.ts) through src/lib/limits-text.ts, with GitHub's
// documentation page as its source.
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";
import { hostingFacts, limitTables, SOURCES } from "../../lib/limits-text";

const t = hostingFacts();
const tables = limitTables();
const pushInParts = [
  "git log --oneline --reverse main",
  "git push origin OLDER-COMMIT:refs/heads/main",
  "git push origin NEWER-COMMIT:refs/heads/main",
  "git push origin main",
];
---
<Base
  title="Limits"
  description="File, push and repository sizes on GitHub, and the registry's own caps."
  breadcrumb={[{ text: "Hosting code", href: "/hosting/" }, { text: "Limits" }]}
>
  <h1>Limits</h1>
  <p class="summary">
    File, push and repository sizes on GitHub, and {SITE_NAME}'s own caps. Your repositories live in your GitHub account,
    so GitHub's limits are the ones that apply; each figure links to GitHub's page that states it.
  </p>
  <p>{t.units}</p>

  <h2 id="file-size">File size</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub warns when you push a file larger than {t.fileWarn}, and blocks a file larger than {t.file}
    ({t.fileInWords}). Its web upload page accepts files up to {t.webUpload}. A larger file belongs in a release asset,
    a data archive or Git LFS: <a href="/hosting/large-files/">the large-files guide</a>.
    Source: <a href={SOURCES.largeFiles}>About large files on GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="push-size">Push size</h2>
  <p>
    One push is at most {t.push} ({t.pushInWords}). A first push of a long history, or of a converted repository, can
    cross it. Source: <a href={SOURCES.repositoryLimits}>Repository limits</a>.
  </p>
  <h3 id="push-limit">{t.pushLimitTitle}</h3>
  <p>
    Push the history in parts, oldest first: list the commits, push an older commit to the branch, then a newer one,
    until the branch is whole. Each part stays under the limit.
  </p>
  <pre class="commands">{pushInParts.join("\n")}</pre>
  <p>Source: <a href={SOURCES.pushLimit}>GitHub's page on the push limit</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="repository-size">Repository size</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub asks that repositories stay small: ideally under {t.repoIdeal}, and under {t.repoStrongly} strongly
    recommended. Research code rarely comes near it; data does, and goes elsewhere
    (<a href="/hosting/large-files/">large files</a>). Source: <a href={SOURCES.repositoryLimits}>Repository limits</a>.
  </p>
  <table class="limits">
    <caption>Sizes on GitHub</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Limit</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {tables.sizes.map((r) => (
        <tr><td>{r.what}</td><td class="num">{r.value}</td><td>{r.source ? <a href={r.source}>GitHub's documentation</a> : SITE_NAME}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="tree-limits">Tree limits</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond these, GitHub's pages and its API slow down or stop listing: keep a directory to fewer entries (a
    folder of many small files is better as one archive in a release), and a repository's branches few.
  </p>
  <table class="limits">
    <caption>Tree limits and repositories per account</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Limit</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {tables.tree.map((r) => (
        <tr><td>{r.what}</td><td class="num">{r.value}</td><td>{r.source ? <a href={r.source}>GitHub's documentation</a> : SITE_NAME}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="push-policy">Push policy</h2>
  <p>
    Branch and tag updates per push: in a repository's settings, GitHub's push policy can limit how many branches and
    tags one push may update. It stops an accidental <code>git push --mirror</code> from overwriting or deleting every
    branch at once. When a push is refused for it, git's output names the setting.
    Source: <a href={SOURCES.pushPolicy}>Managing the push policy for your repository</a>.
  </p>
  <table class="limits">
    <caption>Branch and tag updates per push</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Figure</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {tables.pushPolicy.map((r) => (
        <tr><td>{r.what}</td><td class="num">{r.value}</td><td>{r.source ? <a href={r.source}>GitHub's documentation</a> : SITE_NAME}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3 id="path-exceptions">Push rule path exceptions</h3>
  <p>
    A push ruleset can refuse files by path, extension, path length or size. Since 2026-08-25 the path and size rules
    can exempt chosen paths: a size rule, for example, can let through the small folder of reference data a paper's
    figures are built from. Sources: <a href={SOURCES.availableRules}>Available rules for rulesets</a>,
    <a href={SOURCES.pushRulePathExceptions}>GitHub's changelog</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="registry-caps">{SITE_NAME}'s own caps</h2>
  <p>
    What passes through {SITE_NAME}'s Worker is capped so that the service stays free. A larger commit or asset goes
    through GitHub's own pages or <code>git push</code>, which {SITE_NAME} never sees. The per-account counts are over
    the last {t.capWindow}, and a refusal says when you can try again.
  </p>
  <table class="limits">
    <caption>{SITE_NAME}'s caps</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Cap</th><th>Set by</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {tables.registry.map((r) => (
        <tr><td>{r.what}</td><td class="num">{r.value}</td><td>{r.source ? <a href={r.source}>GitHub's documentation</a> : SITE_NAME}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="billing">Billing</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE_NAME} costs nothing and bills nothing: it asks for no payment method, and it has no plan to sell. What you
    use on GitHub is on your own GitHub account, under GitHub's plan.
  </p>
  {t.lfs && (
    <>
      <p>
        GitHub Free's Git LFS quotas are per account: {t.lfsStorage} of storage and {t.lfsBandwidth} of downloads a
        month. Without a payment method on file, Git LFS is blocked for the account once a quota is spent, until the next
        month; with one, the excess is billed. Source: <a href={SOURCES.lfsBilling}>Git LFS billing</a>.
      </p>
      <table class="limits">
        <caption>GitHub Free's Git LFS quotas</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Limit</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {tables.lfs.map((r) => (
            <tr><td>{r.what}</td><td class="num">{r.value}</td><td>{r.source ? <a href={r.source}>GitHub's documentation</a> : SITE_NAME}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </>
  )}
  <h3 id="budgets">Budgets and usage alerts</h3>
  <p>
    Budgets and usage alerts are GitHub's: you set them on GitHub's billing pages, and GitHub sends the alerts. Usage
    is counted by calendar month. {SITE_NAME} sets no budget and sees none of your usage.
    Sources: <a href={SOURCES.budgets}>Budgets and alerts</a>, <a href={SOURCES.setUpBudgets}>Setting up budgets</a>,
    <a href={SOURCES.billingCycles}>Billing cycles</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pages">GitHub Pages size limits</h2>
  <p>
    A GitHub Pages site built from your repository has its own limits. {SITE_NAME} links a repository's Pages site,
    and never rebuilds or copies it. Source: <a href={SOURCES.pagesLimits}>GitHub Pages limits</a>.
  </p>
  <table class="limits">
    <caption>GitHub Pages</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Limit</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {tables.pages.map((r) => (
        <tr><td>{r.what}</td><td class="num">{r.value}</td><td>{r.source ? <a href={r.source}>GitHub's documentation</a> : SITE_NAME}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p><a href="/hosting/">The hosting guides</a></p>
</Base>
